          %p
          #DiagrammaticeCInavriantReproducedClassicaleCInvariant.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              The diagrammatic
              = succeed "-invariant" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi e
                          %mo ^
                        %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} KU
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widehat e_{\mathrm{KU}}
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LiftedEInvariantDiagrammatically"}
              reproduces the classical Adams
              = succeed "-invariant." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi e
                        %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e_{\mathbb{C}}
          %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              The homotopy-commuting rectangle in the bottom right part of the defining pasting diagram Def.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LiftedEInvariantDiagrammatically"}
              says that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi p
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi e
                        %mo ^
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} KU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mo =
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mo *
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} (
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} ch
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} [
                    %mo ⊢
                    %msubsup
                      %mi H
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} KU
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ]
                    %mo{:maxsize => "1.8em", :minsize => "1.8em"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    p^\ast
                    \\Big(
                    \\widehat e_{\mathrm{KU}}(c)
                    ,
                    [c]
                    \\Big)
                    \\;=\;
                    i_\ast
                    \\Big(
                    \\mathrm{ch}
                    \\big[
                    \\vdash H^{\mathrm{KU}}_{2n-1}(c)
                    \\big]
                    \\Big)
                    \\,.
            %p
              By Lemma
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CompatibilityOfTheTwoCanonicalSplittings"}
              this means that the image of both sides along their canonical retractions (Remark
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CanonicalSplittingOfCofiberOfPeriodicRationalCohomology"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mi d
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2(n+d)
              rational cohomology
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi ℚ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \simeq \mathbb{Q}
              coincide. But by Definition
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LiftedEInvariantDiagrammatically"}
              and Remark
              = succeed "," do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#eCInvaiantViaCanonicalSplitting"}
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℚ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Q}
            %p
          %p
            %br/
          %h3#ConnerFloydTheoremViaPastingDiagram Recovering Conner-Floyd’s e-invariant
          %p
            With this diagrammatic formulation of the e-invariant, the Conner-Floyd theorem – that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/e-invariant+is+the+Todd+class+of+cobounding+%28U%2Cfr%29-manifolds"} e-invariant is the Todd class of cobounding (U,fr)-manifolds
            – follows as an immediate corollary: It just amounts to factoring the above pasting composite further through
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MU"} MU
          %svg{:height => "304.564", :viewbox => "0 0 365.362 304.564", :width => "365.362", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
            %defs
              %g
